ield/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160948/selection/0d2e574246b3098fd0c6da879fa829baa6ef57be893baf7b239e9349e2dea075" :answer-field/choices ["q-160948/selection/42868c6be1e39b19df616f741fc53e780928a99ce80c150ebf7f47e06ba2aadb" "q-160948/selection/0d2e574246b3098fd0c6da879fa829baa6ef57be893baf7b239e9349e2dea075" "q-160948/selection/a90aa396b4ff9ec18fff24209b24709477c21a51ae5fc1acd9741500ad836c69" "q-160948/selection/7d0deaa08be4198b03b74ccc0c57922b7c7b27ee69aa40e2135f96e1f3494451" "q-160948/selection/54f69ce46dbbb42b0142293f1ecfc2209dec9d0bb960b74c28fa013aadcf9df6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160948" :question/id #uuid "29377ed0-f103-5d84-ae34-1182af962bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-160948" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/930509fb9224152be07ef3e14550e0f7afe803428a357963eb3796e0650685a3.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the second and third quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160948/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160948"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160945/selection/36682c5f37b64762730b04d5ae8376fee561dbd3c99a707027fc9b58b8039d5b" :answer/id #uuid "e0355357-5b11-5cce-b549-78d9d67bf027" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/2be15ae2d8bf7a309065ac33c97f5937d3fc8c12c5ca6ddc0c54ab44f555a5d1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160945/selection/fd31793a13e3f3762af9bb37afdf4cb7aadc3bef513e22b9507509b8aca1bb34" :answer/id #uuid "690d666e-d29a-5566-931e-adabf743b927" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/b611546ee3a8ae2714de1cc97dec62b9c750a39f858864b63d70be0d26a65b68.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160945/selection/e7bfe3651fc287a348bc80d7129b50eeea5d384caf5468c1c0dea04776f58b04" :answer/id #uuid "b2dece5a-40cc-576f-80d0-0ca8ea25b267" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/9a40e3dbc6eeecaf9b33830b762e75f54d7ffea5c1dd9ab9ae6e2bd9cca721e6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160945/selection/e506936c5ac9ffbf24366c16fd08219b