uld be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/b829ff7e7a51ed9f8089b0b8fc22f727ccf5dc7b2e937ec90f6a1affb8d29064.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-160945/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160945"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160944/selection/e23a7a171089bd0b23af3ea532558df354ddc7422401d77ba653f358f85e8a3a" :answer/id #uuid "c1a0d58a-0583-5d6f-b396-1eea0cd9f441" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/df47e830c98a8f5e3ddfdf909ec23163735b21486eb25d784bb6b88ea95e4f60.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160944/selection/a90aa396b4ff9ec18fff24209b24709477c21a51ae5fc1acd9741500ad836c69" :answer/id #uuid "66b3a447-46c3-5695-8fa2-c58df3df739a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The 