g $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})),\\,0\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161026/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161026"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160914/selection/024bf776617df1a78bb590858001a21cef590e52add434d34cec0e04f66fd74d" :answer/id #uuid "d79c9166-24ff-51a2-b424-c41a56512d9b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/5adf324eba50123bd9a6dc3f27be38a7db0b4835bac8479e705f696abcf404b4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160914/selection/04f1093c58a44f42aac46bfbd3ccbbf9ff14291b43212056dacf082eb6b74c70" :answer/id #uuid "fd09bd09-4823-57ce-9a6f-39e3e8ceeb39" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/39ba02dcd51d54bf28a84e19786d763c201858979c45f65a11069b7ba4d0584d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160914/selection/182ca09a555255461e37f9d6d233216818d3031984dc4512c13184ab564eb035" :answer/id #uuid "dbf9d8df-a42b-5788-add4-85bba83b71a8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/5263c0c82be5ab6b0290e5017943c7c2584d3bd23783c5420e38af2df618f9b0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160914/selection/e38686e4da1b54fd83f11f8d125ed1bbd95d87552fde8f9ab4b00247a3962f14" :answer/id #uuid "74e1f549-07b2-5d9a-a0da-1f907b9ba356" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/25ec9c36f1e6637a0034cdae142977b4a4b5c3237471ef93f4bf55c6f4ea86b7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160914/selection/8e84dfd57bcae15ea14ecfb33c7380c2fa54d9c0e61e9ab175138071be8f579d" :answer/id #uuid "2a301fbe-ac55-53bc-9fd4-1320aea78a12" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/0799c923b5e7061f8a6ec1faf9ed8b73038dfe4c1b63607a8e037d66d6ce88fd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160914/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "70815d54-9fff-5916-8240-6134755ba69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160914/selection/04f1093c58a44f42aac46bfbd3ccbbf9ff14291b43212056dacf082eb6b74c70" :answer-field/choices ["q-160914/selection/024bf776617df1a78bb590858001a21cef590e52add434d34cec0e04f66fd74d" "q-160914/selection/04f1093c58a44f42aac46bfbd3ccbbf9ff14291b43212056dacf082eb6b74c70" "q-160914/selection/182ca09a555255461e37f9d6d233216818d3031984dc4512c13184ab564eb035" "q-160914/selection/e38686e4da1b54fd83f11f8d125ed1bbd95d87552fde8f9ab4b00247a3962f14" "q-160914/selection/8e84dfd57bcae15ea14ecfb33c7380c2fa54d9c0e61e9ab175138071be8f579d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160914" :question/id #uuid "cdd9fff8-ea20-509c-a6da-935754b92421" :question/math-academy-id "q-160914" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/3621e70552c3891f43521e74704d5db3f276f9928669390d688cca1bc3449217.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c8ad4cf64e9ea17b225373d8ed238c8588c0b6f45577d2ca15b22ede5e34d7d8.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160914/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160914"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160909/selection/76d3af96e83c0fa793ad00eac7355791df8a867491faf1467a01fb4615930b81" :answer/id #uuid "59a0127c-58f1-59af-b252-8721815768d0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/128955fddc503b24eb73c5a321a803a4318f16e83e88f1d28be0a0e621f926c9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160909/selection/061d4f530148552be5de52ec0ceb7a3b64613467002964bc1074e4b148046e70" :answer/id #uuid "44fdb6c4-5db1-5225-acf5-6c057e7df861" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/709f9c571b81383cb436570ea4c63575a2fb7e83b1c2e377a6347f54c8ceea35.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160909/selection/f6a4198c4a926b2d413467dba9f02b4ef1e8f06b613f9918949877882a2a03b9" :answer/id #uuid "7ac40caf-e9b8-5257-a8fc-9bc5350ae5f2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/82557cc5a33963a7ec6d7910da8dc1409fd2cdc1245b00be009f7a237bee6c3a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160909/selection/900f0269304ba7a404d3d3475794221620758ca466ad7521ac1bd2392fe3c374" :answer/id #uuid "30baa49c-27f7-5be4-a36e-e4f9739a0877" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1b2026b5c41f64f72aa9d1c849f9357f4192860545e9a41241c2a0f87d33db6a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160909/selection/b9f3485f3e8750253773ace71e2c5b7db5d3f24ef74fb99b5325c06af5dba5b5" :answer/id #uuid "9abe1e9f-e58d-5073-ba46-76f90e5db6e9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160909/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "55c9ca47-9a76-55ad-a2d3-88696863f96a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160909/selection/b9f3485f3e8750253773ace71e2c5b7db5d3f24ef74fb99b5325c06af5dba5b5" :answer-field/choices ["q-160909/selection/76d3af96e83c0fa793ad00eac7355791df8a867491faf1467a01fb4615930b81" "q-160909/selection/061d4f530148552be5de52ec0ceb7a3b64613467002964bc1074e4b148046e70" "q-160909/selection/f6a4198c4a926b2d413467dba9f02b4ef1e8f06b613f9918949877882a2a03b9" "q-160909/selection/900f0269304ba7a404d3d3475794221620758ca466ad7521ac1bd2392fe3c374" "q-160909/selection/b9f3485f3e8750253773ace71e2c5b7db5d3f24ef74fb99b5325c06af5dba5b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160909" :question/id #uuid "31d74e75-fb66-5eb8-9774-1cd2896eeaa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160909" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160909/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-poi