ider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{2},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-160912/selection/d3827302d735adcd8714211286418ca73573ae6dedd309a39bad6befaa117531" :answer/id #uuid "0e7dad81-7a6f-507f-a9ad-a6318d00080d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160