gle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160925/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160925"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160951/selection/9087a6497d9aca78d1723c38d9b2098f507bd03b7c5b070cdb8f3be2b4ed0b08" :answer/id #uuid "9776820f-0d74-553d-bc56-faafddc977fe" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/7feba2657a73ffe233cb78cdc8b63634c3af67ab64e517f1ae97319c89b0e757.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160951/selection/9c2bbda5ded2502a38af281cecd658c86f0bb80e1cad877e8b06a18c1b3923e2" :answer/id #uuid "b1184bc5-5861-52ef-854f-7c40821f03d4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/4440fc795798064ebaec0a41ec89d156ab16e86672b7d467acb197c50acfb0f8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160951/selection/594445845b7209dcf7012f6f4533a824fbe90f6dd6bb01022c92bee13ae58a94" :answer/id #uuid "158c25ae-0fec-5f92-aeee-6758d781029b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/e42a5506af4f1d8638dc09938217bb1f6e059d94945af11c847b63753ed768c7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160951/selection/afe789ffee019400eaf78ba2d4212f438803b6a563f3996cf6bde35fae94b216" :answer/id #uuid "06531186-d811-5ca1-9c07-9ccc1e319bf2" :answer/ty