:answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161113/selection/fdbe1006845651475d207d15334e0bf30c11db5840da5b4d3c4a1189a7cd8f85" :answer-field/choices ["q-161113/selection/64b147331d0397350ce8ae2aee1d27e76de31e1b7e3f28c7eed585f585fef2c6" "q-161113/selection/92d2203b0ebe2b3ba8f16d3698a38cf568ed81727b3d4e261987f71e5289a08b" "q-161113/selection/e370e1d4e45debc9fc145bf805bf1990b1ab433a95455b09f9a52e77a1efe163" "q-161113/selection/fdbe1006845651475d207d15334e0bf30c11db5840da5b4d3c4a1189a7cd8f85" "q-161113/selection/b72e0f75a7598354b93c0b8a43f3f96bf18f07f2f8aad889e979256987fb05b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161113" :question/id #uuid "dc6f6478-43eb-5df8-9592-d3846d859923" :question/math-academy-id "q-161113" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/22ed424a27d1674bc7f4316b0b644e3dbac4c9f460e23ea2aa6ee90cb74b4c5b.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161113/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161113"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161118/selection/5d9c9d6a4f4783aa11d1c10f267bc0e2bdd73a97e0bd3b9773094257ea468b23" :answer/id #uuid "412b6db1-a753-5a88-9b81-22e8566b2047" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161118/selection/4039068ec75611ce7bbd90760003c55f16b97ad8b558bd2ddc8ea51d77aeca49" :answer/id #uuid "f1b2f3fa-2bee-5922-914e-95a6284f5dda" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/e6239ef3eb407b37db7029088b6671af710b38f81622735087205bfae0bde67f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161118/selection/2adc6edb868121156e772ddbb8e1d6461be9c34d27b009ff7e2a75dc65e1ff61" :answer/id #uuid "a6311e30-21f7-5c5e-9328-735070425f5e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c8662ff0f722b1c0b651bc35291242713ca57c271dc1011be75d2217e3f95f3c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161118/selection/e3ae712427f3c04d47e7ca84c6c0a6b0a840d9478814e7217a94944e6a58b076" :answer/id #uuid "fd8affcd-4ee0-5aba-bd25-f33a719494e7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/f5cb3c952c7346f6457e082b3f815eca8e6e8f4e4d54ea96448977f2cfc4d7e6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161118/selection/4d7eabf9ab1ec5b7cc17bfffed482ff8872b671a9b387177b451dfd11de07ac8" :answer/id #uuid "33b961cf-db4f-5cfb-95fe-320579eddca9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ff3ef277236f696894e85fef3cd7c2cff227f5a1844628d5f34fb613644b43d2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161118/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebdfb9de-36b7-5921-b9f6-81c1d1860f2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161118/selection/5d9c9d6a4f4783aa11d1c10f267bc0e2bdd73a97e0bd3b9773094257ea468b23" :answer-field/choices ["q-161118/selection/5d9c9d6a4f4783aa11d1c10f267bc0e2bdd73a97e0bd3b9773094257ea468b23" "q-161118/selection/4039068ec75611ce7bbd90760003c55f16b97ad8b558bd2ddc8ea51d77aeca49" "q-161118/selection/2adc6edb868121156e772ddbb8e1d6461be9c34d27b009ff7e2a75dc65e1ff61" "q-161118/selection/e3ae712427f3c04d47e7ca84c6c0a6b0a840d9478814e7217a94944e6a58b076" "q-161118/selection/4d7eabf9ab1ec5b7cc17bfffed482ff8872b671a9b387177b451dfd11de07ac8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161118" :question/id #uuid "070ee7ae-92db-5231-ab62-571cfac8cde2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161118" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/8360f06b0d9e9633aa00b779702d386cd403d7fe8ac8a28b51922dca92ddafed.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=0$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161118/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161118"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-11153"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/93c82014d308d19ebfc10e7046ac1e8b6cf78017b6bc10e0671f43d22aac996a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the right side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u = 2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u = 2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/8b14a175f7931dda3e3b6afd97c5219c390a0674f147289a257e0e655dcc11f1.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12363"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/86a240b5c7c1fc945e12da274be7e9cac14c1b5a9add004d52d72390afb39aff.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the top side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,1\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/81650cc0dc43537ec916f2c68af66ed5581c861812f276dda2b23baccc2fb491.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12340"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/6515550b9a280f423cdc2a2e89fd83014733c0783fdd4b2b2966e6a4dd37492a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/79869cf64ec827934397588e25d06cba0c9d12c35ecbea5cdd6aaf6cfcf12e24.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12332"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/1a869685bd7163515672154b20c2a41a50b5df320bae30e650d7a6d0b7d44ee1.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v) , \\, y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y= u \\sin v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $\\Delta$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Sketch the region $\\Delta.$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y = u \\sin v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $\\Delta$ of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  \\sqrt 2 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt 2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/c83bd9d913d4959ad275ab4420b647cd030fff45438f59d026e3658bd146a1ee.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
